{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791904759,"nonce":"EB1lPT9StgI9xJME86V+KAxMZBA=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABW6u1bg","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/166","origin":"github"},"title":"fem: a boundary term summed into the volume term is silently integrated over the volume","message":"A boundary term written into the same expression as the volume term is assembled as a **volume** integral,\nwith no error or warning. The answer is silently wrong.\n\n## Repro\n\n`-ΔT = 1` on the unit cube, adiabatic everywhere except a Robin condition `h T = flux` on `z = 0` (`h = 2`).\nExact: `T(z) = 1/h + z - z²/2`, so `T(0) = 0.5`, `T(1) = 1.0`.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np, jax, jno\njax.config.update(\"jax_enable_x64\", True)\nd = jno.shape.box(0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1).structured(n=(3, 3, 8)).quad().domain(compute_mesh_connectivity=False)\nT, v = d.fem_symbols()\nxi, yi, zi = d.variable(\"interior\", split=True)[:3]\nxb, yb, zb = d.variable(\"bottom\", split=True)[:3]\nTi, vi = T.bind(x=xi, y=yi, z=zi), v.bind(x=xi, y=yi, z=zi)\nTb, vb = T.bind(x=xb, y=yb, z=zb), v.bind(x=xb, y=yb, z=zb)\nvol = Ti.x * vi.x + Ti.y * vi.y + Ti.z * vi.z - 1.0 * vi\nrob = 2.0 * Tb * vb\np = np.asarray(d.mesh.points)\nfor name, terms in ((\"separate\", [vol, rob]), (\"combined\", [vol + rob])):\n    fem = jno.fem(terms)\n    u = np.asarray(fem.solve())\n    print(name, fem.classification, u[p[:, 2] \u003c 1e-9].mean(), u[p[:, 2] \u003e 1 - 1e-9].mean())\n```\n\n| terms | classification | T(z=0) | T(z=1) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `[vol, rob]` | `['volume', 'surface@bottom']` | 0.5000 | 1.0000 (exact) |\n| `[vol + rob]` | `['volume']` | 0.5000 | 0.5000 (wrong) |\n\nIn the combined form, `h T v` is integrated over the volume. That gives `T = f/h` everywhere, and the diffusion\nterm no longer matters. The same happens on tetrahedra (`.structured()` without `.quad()`) and with a tag\nmade by `d.tag(...)` in place of the named face. It was found on a layered 3-D heat-conduction model, where it\ngave temperatures about 100x too high.\n\n## Expected\n\nEither split the sum into its volume and surface parts by the region each addend is bound to, or refuse a\nterm whose addends are bound to different regions and name the fix (\"give the boundary term its own list\nentry\"). The docs (\"a term's region is carried by the symbols you bind into it\") suggest the first.\n\nTested on 1b42314.","files":null}]}